# /etc/conf.d/nccl — the single source of NCCL environment for BareCortex
# training. Every consumer resolves NCCL_* from here; nothing else.
#
# Scope: single node. There is no cluster here, and anything that exists
# only for multi-node operation (InfiniBand, remote ifname) does not belong
# in this file — its absence is a statement, not a missing setting.
#
# Sourcing rules (design D8):
# - This file assigns without exporting, per conf.d convention. A
#   non-exported assignment never reaches the training process, so every
#   consumer wraps the source:
#
#       set -a
#       . /etc/conf.d/nccl
#       set +a
#
#   Keep the three lines adjacent: anything assigned between them is
#   exported, and only that.
# - OpenRC auto-sources conf.d/<name> only for the service named <name>.
#   There is no service called "nccl", so nothing is sourced here
#   implicitly and every consumer sources the file explicitly. The cpufreq
#   service, by contrast, is named after its conf.d file and needs no
#   source line.
# - An OpenRC service is not a login shell: /etc/profile.d never reaches
#   it, which is why the init script sources this file itself instead of
#   relying on the profile.d shim.
#
# Tuning ledger (6.9): one row per variable below, naming the measurement
# that justifies it and the baseline it beat. A variable with no row is
# removed. A default is not a finding — NCCL's own default is the baseline,
# and a line restating it reads like a decision while changing nothing:
#
#   # NCCL_DEBUG=WARN  # no-op: WARN is already the default; no measurement
#
# (No variables currently: nothing on this node has been measured against a
# baseline. The network variables this file used to carry in its consumers —
# NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME, NCCL_IB_DISABLE, NCCL_SHM_DISABLE, NCCL_NET_GDR_LEVEL,
# NCCL_P2P_LEVEL — were guesses about hardware not yet checked on this
# machine, and a guess with no row is removed.)
